# Development

## Prerequisites

Go {{ .Service.GoVersion }}, Docker, SSH access to `{{ .Service.CoreModule }}`
(`export GOPRIVATE={{ .Service.CoreModule }}`), optionally `rover` for `make supergraph`.

## Everyday commands

| Command | Does |
|---|---|
| `make setup` | Installs dlv, creates `config/local.yaml` from the example, downloads modules |
| `make db-up` / `db-down` / `db-reset` | Local Postgres in docker on :{{ .Service.DBPort }} |
| `make migrate-up` / `migrate-down N=1` | Apply / roll back migrations against `config/local.yaml` |
| `make migrate-new NAME=add_x` | Next numbered empty migration pair |
| `make serve` | Run with `config/local.yaml` |
| `make debug` | Run under dlv on :2345; attach with the "attach to make debug" VS Code config |
| `make gql MODULE=<module>` | Regenerate one module's GraphQL code |
| `make test` | Unit tests, no database |
| `make test-integration` | Unit + integration tests against `{{ .Service.DBName }}_test` |
| `make e2e` | Only the end-to-end tests (`TestE2E*`): real handlers, real Postgres, every operation over HTTP |
| `make cover` | Everything, failing below 100% coverage of hand-written code |
| `make smoke` | Probe a running server |

## Tests

- **Unit** tests sit next to the code and use hand-written stubs of the layer below (no mock generators).
- **Integration** tests carry `//go:build integration`. Each package's `TestMain` calls
  `internal/testutil/pgtest`, which migrates `TEST_DATABASE_DSN` once and gives every test its own
  namespace, so tests never clean up and never collide.
- **End-to-end** tests (`TestE2E*`) drive the real GraphQL handler or gin routes over HTTP with
  pass-through auth.
- **Coverage**: `scripts/coverage.sh` excludes only `graphql/generated` and `internal/testutil`.

## Adding an entity or a module

Use `/devkit:bootstrap`. By hand, the order is: migration → model → DTO → repository →
service (+ allow-list) → mapper → schema + `make gql` → GraphQL mapper + resolver → REST controller →
factories → tests at every layer → module README.
